# Git workflow (trunk-based)

Canonical guide: `docs/git-workflow.md`. Decision: ADR 0043.

## Always

- **`main` is protected.** Never commit, push, or merge directly to `main`. All integration goes through a Pull Request from a short-lived branch.
- **Create a feature branch first** before any code, docs, CI, or config change intended for `main`.
- **Branch names** follow [Conventional Branch](https://conventionalbranch.org/): `<type>/<description>` in lowercase with hyphens only.
  - Use `feature/` or `feat/`, `fix/` or `bugfix/`, `hotfix/`, `release/`, `chore/`.
  - Agent work from Cursor: prefer `cursor/<description>`. Other agents: `copilot/`, `claude/`, `codex/`, or generic `ai/`.
  - Do not invent unprefixed names (e.g. `fase3-wordpress`) for new branches.
- **Commit messages** follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/) in **English**: `type(scope): imperative summary`.
- **Before opening a PR:** run the relevant local gates (`npm run lint:css` for CSS; `composer test` + `composer lint:phpcs` for PHP).
- **Every PR needs at least one relevant GitHub label**; add multiple when the change spans more than one area (e.g. `enhancement` + `documentation`). See `docs/git-workflow.md` § PR labels.
- **Merge only when** GitHub checks `php` and `css` pass and review threads are resolved.

## Examples

```bash
git checkout main && git pull
git checkout -b feature/payload-hash-bookkeeping
git commit -m "fix(migrate): exclude _source_key from content hash"
gh pr create --base main --label bug
```

## Do not

- Do not push to `main`, even for “small” or docs-only fixes.
- Do not open a PR without at least one relevant label.
- Do not use branch names with uppercase, underscores, spaces, or consecutive hyphens.
- Do not use non-conventional commit subjects (`updated stuff`, `WIP`, `fix bug`).
